1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,240
And now for you Scoutmasters.

2
00:00:08,080 --> 00:00:09,420
So tell me this:

3
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:15,300
How are Scoutmasters different from Sherlock Holmes?

4
00:00:17,740 --> 00:00:20,540
Well, Sherlock Holmes occasionally had a clue, didn't he?

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:25,380
Yeah, alrighty then.

6
00:00:25,920 --> 00:00:27,700
Hey, this is podcast number 39..

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:31,960
Hey,

8
00:00:56,460 --> 00:00:58,160
Welcome back to the Scoutmaster Podcast.

9
00:00:58,420 --> 00:00:59,100
This is Clark Green.

10
00:00:59,100 --> 00:01:01,560
Hey, we just got back from the camporee. this past weekend

11
00:01:02,060 --> 00:01:12,540
Had a big council camporee with, you know, 1,500 of our closest friends, in the middle of a big field at a county park.

12
00:01:13,100 --> 00:01:14,480
Saturday, a beautiful day.

13
00:01:15,180 --> 00:01:22,520
The wind was probably about 20 miles an hour, to you know, 350 miles an hour, gusts all day.

14
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:25,479
I'm exaggerating a little bit.

15
00:01:25,480 --> 00:01:32,120
Camporees present me with the unusual opportunity, as a Scoutmaster, of learning to keep my mouth shut.

16
00:01:33,160 --> 00:01:43,100
You know, when you're in a big field- and you have, you know your square of campsite is laid out for you and you set up your tents, you're going to be a few steps away from the Scouts most of the time,

17
00:01:43,100 --> 00:01:49,339
And our habit is to make sure that we set up patrol sites and we're a good distance away from the Scouts.

18
00:01:49,740 --> 00:01:57,060
And when you're right there, man, you see every little thing and you want to jump in every single time and offer some advice,

19
00:01:57,060 --> 00:02:03,000
Do a little mentoring perhaps, or just kind of yell at them every once in a while.

20
00:02:05,320 --> 00:02:07,060
So it was a bit of a challenge for me.

21
00:02:07,400 --> 00:02:13,160
Sat there with my assistant Scoutmasters and we tried to talk and not pay too much attention.

22
00:02:14,120 --> 00:02:16,200
It's not that the Scouts do things wrong,

23
00:02:16,580 --> 00:02:19,220
It's just. you know, do I have to explain

24
00:02:19,220 --> 00:02:30,320
This time around? I'm going to talk to you about a parents meeting that we have coming up, an annual parents meeting in court of honor. I'm going to explain

25
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:31,940
And then we're going to talk a little bit about ceremony.

26
00:02:32,060 --> 00:02:33,440
That'll wrap it up for us today.

27
00:02:34,680 --> 00:02:36,700
So we've got some things to go with.

28
00:02:37,520 --> 00:02:38,860
Let's get started, shall we

29
00:02:49,220 --> 00:02:52,780
Boy Scouts?

30
00:02:59,840 --> 00:03:04,480
So I'm preparing right now for our annual parents meeting. that's going to happen tonight

31
00:03:04,480 --> 00:03:13,260
And I thought it would make a decent podcast to just kind of go through some of the ideas that we apply to that meeting and exactly what we cover.

32
00:03:13,260 --> 00:03:25,040
This meeting is combined with a court of honor that will recognize Scouts for all of the achievements that they have earned since our last court of honor, which is usually about somewhere between four and six months in the past,

33
00:03:26,440 --> 00:03:32,079
And we ask each family to make sure that there is at least one parent present at this meeting,

34
00:03:32,239 --> 00:03:35,260
And this meeting will last from a half hour to 45 minutes.

35
00:03:35,940 --> 00:03:42,320
At the meeting. we'll be talking about some of the administrative things that we need to do as a Scout troop.

36
00:03:43,260 --> 00:03:49,380
It's an excellent time for everyone to recharter or renew their membership for the coming year.

37
00:03:49,739 --> 00:03:52,079
We'll talk about our outdoor program plan.

38
00:03:52,380 --> 00:03:53,859
We'll talk about Friends of Scouting

39
00:03:54,160 --> 00:04:00,880
And we also have a group camping gear order that we do on an annual basis

40
00:04:01,380 --> 00:04:05,019
And we present that during that meeting.

41
00:04:06,299 --> 00:04:11,200
I know that I'm going to just jump around a little bit and talk about the various aspects of the meeting.

42
00:04:11,200 --> 00:04:17,000
One of them is the Friends of Scouting, which is making direct contributions to support scouting in your council,

43
00:04:18,399 --> 00:04:22,580
And I have done several of these presentations in the past,

44
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:27,500
So I'll usually go ahead and take that part of the meeting.

45
00:04:27,500 --> 00:04:32,260
I like to tell folks this:

46
00:04:32,380 --> 00:04:36,640
I'll give you just kind of an encapsulated version of the way this presentation works,

47
00:04:37,280 --> 00:04:42,260
Because I volunteer with our borough planning commission, our local municipal government

48
00:04:43,020 --> 00:04:46,960
And you know, every once in a while I will hear somebody complain about their water bill

49
00:04:46,960 --> 00:04:51,099
And what I like to point out to them is that their water is free.

50
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:54,000
It's just the pipes that cost money.

51
00:04:54,419 --> 00:05:01,180
I mean the system that makes water safe to drink and keeps the water mains flowing and treats wastewater. that's what costs money.

52
00:05:01,219 --> 00:05:03,020
The water itself, that's free.

53
00:05:03,659 --> 00:05:07,539
Your water meter measures the load you put on the system and we charge accordingly.

54
00:05:07,540 --> 00:05:11,280
And scouting, like water, is free.

55
00:05:12,180 --> 00:05:16,440
Books, badges, uniforms and tents and food cost money,

56
00:05:16,780 --> 00:05:20,500
But the camaraderie, achievement and adventure of scouting are all free.

57
00:05:21,200 --> 00:05:28,160
The vast majority of people who deliver scouting, the places we meet and even some of the places we camp, come with no charge.

58
00:05:28,160 --> 00:05:34,180
We do have a small number of full-time professionals in what's called a council,

59
00:05:34,900 --> 00:05:40,640
And the council maintains a service center and a 3,000-acre scout reservation

60
00:05:41,300 --> 00:05:45,380
And these all cost money to maintain and they're vital to delivering scouting.

61
00:05:45,840 --> 00:05:49,040
Each are really important parts of a virtual pipeline.

62
00:05:49,560 --> 00:05:57,180
Now most people are not going to visit their wastewater treatment plant or not going to think very much about the system that delivers water to their home.

63
00:05:57,180 --> 00:06:02,600
You go in the kitchen, you turn on the faucet and there's water that's going to be there.

64
00:06:03,200 --> 00:06:07,400
And most parents will never see the network of professionals who support scouting.

65
00:06:08,920 --> 00:06:14,820
They simply expect scouting, like water, to be there when they turn on the tap.

66
00:06:15,240 --> 00:06:18,320
Delivering the promises of scouting does cost money.

67
00:06:19,860 --> 00:06:23,340
Water meters read the load that you've put on the system

68
00:06:23,340 --> 00:06:27,560
and what you owe to keep the delivery system maintained.

69
00:06:28,760 --> 00:06:34,219
And what I'm asking you to do for friends of scouting is to measure what your son gets from scouting

70
00:06:34,219 --> 00:06:39,780
and to give a really good idea of trying to translate that into dollars and cents.

71
00:06:40,320 --> 00:06:41,440
And that's what you owe.

72
00:06:41,919 --> 00:06:44,620
That's what you owe to help maintain our council.

73
00:06:45,039 --> 00:06:47,039
That's what you owe to help maintain our camp.

74
00:06:47,040 --> 00:06:51,760
I know there are associated fees with some of the things that we do, but these are all at cost.

75
00:06:52,440 --> 00:06:55,160
These are exactly what we are being charged for them

76
00:06:55,760 --> 00:07:00,140
And often, because of our fundraising efforts, we're able to underwrite those

77
00:07:00,140 --> 00:07:04,780
and save a substantial amount of money over a year's time.

78
00:07:05,320 --> 00:07:10,920
So that's a nutshell of the way that I present friends of scouting during this parents' meeting.

79
00:07:11,060 --> 00:07:15,380
As I mentioned, we also do a group camping gear order every year.

80
00:07:15,380 --> 00:07:19,020
Now understand, this podcast is not sponsored.

81
00:07:19,400 --> 00:07:23,560
It doesn't have anyone paying me to produce it every week.

82
00:07:23,719 --> 00:07:33,360
I do have a couple of affiliate programs that run ads on the Scoutmaster blog, at scoutmastertypepadcom,

83
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:37,460
And that brings in a very tiny amount of money. basically,

84
00:07:37,640 --> 00:07:40,960
that defers most of the cost of producing the podcast.

85
00:07:40,960 --> 00:07:42,180
But I don't have sponsors,

86
00:07:42,180 --> 00:07:45,219
And I say that because I'm going to mention a business name

87
00:07:45,380 --> 00:07:53,780
And I want you to understand that this is coming to you from me without any kind of money being involved

88
00:07:53,780 --> 00:07:55,740
or sponsorship or anything like that.

89
00:07:55,800 --> 00:07:58,260
I'd actually love them to sponsor the podcast,

90
00:07:58,860 --> 00:08:00,860
And that business is Campmore,

91
00:08:01,200 --> 00:08:03,060
And many of you may be familiar with it,

92
00:08:03,120 --> 00:08:08,120
But Campmore is a big, giant camping warehouse company that is in Paramus, New Jersey,

93
00:08:08,120 --> 00:08:09,740
And that's not too far from us,

94
00:08:09,740 --> 00:08:19,400
And they historically, as far as I'm concerned, have had some of the best deals on gear that we've been able to find.

95
00:08:19,560 --> 00:08:25,020
Plus, they offer scout troops, as do many other camping outlets and things like that. if you ask,

96
00:08:25,140 --> 00:08:30,440
They offer a 10% discount to a scout troop who orders with a troop. check

97
00:08:30,440 --> 00:08:41,360
What we've done over time- I guess now it's been 10 or 12 years- we have identified the pieces of gear that we know are time-tested and that serve our scouts very well.

98
00:08:41,500 --> 00:08:43,340
So that's a backpack,

99
00:08:43,640 --> 00:08:44,800
That's cooking gear,

100
00:08:44,940 --> 00:08:45,640
That's a compass,

101
00:08:46,060 --> 00:08:47,400
That's a sleeping bag,

102
00:08:47,820 --> 00:08:48,900
That's a sleeping mat.

103
00:08:48,900 --> 00:08:57,680
These are all the things that if we just put them on a list and handed them to parents, you know they would go out and they would do their best to find what looks like it would work.

104
00:08:57,760 --> 00:09:02,680
They might spend a little more money than they would if they got a discount with us,

105
00:09:03,760 --> 00:09:12,900
But it makes it simple for our parents and our scouts to be able to get gear that we know they're going to need for one thing

106
00:09:12,900 --> 00:09:21,560
and that we know really, really works and is well-made and will stand the test of time and that a scout can use pretty much throughout his scouting career.

107
00:09:22,180 --> 00:09:31,560
So we'll spend some time going through our order to camp more and showing everyone the gear and explaining why it's important

108
00:09:32,420 --> 00:09:42,240
And then, after we've dispensed with all of our business and things like that, well then we will open our court of honor,

109
00:09:42,240 --> 00:09:49,920
And I wanted to talk for a few minutes about courts of honor and ceremonies in scouting.

110
00:09:50,600 --> 00:09:56,640
So, without further ado, Scout Mastership in seven minutes or less.

111
00:10:03,720 --> 00:10:06,500
Ceremony is an important part of scouting.

112
00:10:06,640 --> 00:10:08,220
It's something that we shouldn't underestimate.

113
00:10:08,220 --> 00:10:17,940
The formal public presentation and recognition of scouts and their achievements are an opportunity to emphasize exactly what we're all about.

114
00:10:18,040 --> 00:10:24,080
It's a time for people to think about what scouting is doing and what the intentions are.

115
00:10:25,060 --> 00:10:27,500
I'm going to talk mostly about courts of honor here.

116
00:10:28,320 --> 00:10:31,120
They shouldn't be slapdash and just kind of off the cuff,

117
00:10:31,120 --> 00:10:37,880
But in the same way they shouldn't be so constrained and formal that people are kind of vaguely uncomfortable.

118
00:10:39,220 --> 00:10:42,000
Courts of honor are for scouts

119
00:10:42,440 --> 00:10:46,140
And they really need to speak to scouts, not just their leaders or their parents.

120
00:10:47,300 --> 00:10:51,360
The tone we set is one of honoring achievement in a formal way.

121
00:10:51,360 --> 00:11:00,420
If our scouts are kind of getting bored and laughing up their sleeves at an overblown ceremony or some kind of florid theatrical display,

122
00:11:00,700 --> 00:11:04,180
it may be time to rethink the way that we approach a court of honor.

123
00:11:05,080 --> 00:11:09,880
A court of honor isn't just a slog through calling names and handing out a baggie full of badges.

124
00:11:10,960 --> 00:11:13,340
If it is, it's missing something.

125
00:11:13,340 --> 00:11:20,400
My experience has been that scouts appreciate a few things:

126
00:11:20,960 --> 00:11:22,040
They appreciate tradition,

127
00:11:22,640 --> 00:11:24,000
They appreciate brevity

128
00:11:24,560 --> 00:11:26,260
And they appreciate meaning

129
00:11:27,480 --> 00:11:33,880
Ceremonies outside of scouting, whether religious or secular. I mean whatever kind of a ceremony you might go to,

130
00:11:34,440 --> 00:11:36,240
follow an established framework

131
00:11:37,020 --> 00:11:39,240
And within that framework it gets personalized

132
00:11:39,560 --> 00:11:42,020
And what I'm talking about is something like a commencement ceremony.

133
00:11:42,020 --> 00:11:49,560
If you go to a commencement ceremony- for I guess they do them for kindergartners now as well as college graduates-

134
00:11:49,939 --> 00:11:52,280
there are going to be some common elements to the ceremony.

135
00:11:52,380 --> 00:11:57,760
If you go to a wedding- you know as different as weddings can be- there are going to be some common elements.

136
00:11:57,900 --> 00:12:01,340
There's a touchstone there culturally for everybody to latch on to

137
00:12:01,600 --> 00:12:05,300
And we need to think about that when we're putting together our courts of honor.

138
00:12:05,300 --> 00:12:12,000
I would say that, above all, brevity is the most important thing.

139
00:12:13,099 --> 00:12:24,199
You need to be formal and meaningful, without being overly blown, and accomplish the ceremony in somewhere between 30 and 45 minutes,

140
00:12:24,939 --> 00:12:30,800
Not by rushing through it, but by making sure that everything in the ceremony has meaning.

141
00:12:30,800 --> 00:12:32,680
Scouts appreciate simplicity.

142
00:12:33,060 --> 00:12:34,240
Simplicity is memorable

143
00:12:34,819 --> 00:12:36,260
And complexity is aggravating.

144
00:12:37,040 --> 00:12:41,740
So if we're going to explain something, it needs to be explained simply and clearly

145
00:12:42,459 --> 00:12:47,560
And it needs to frame the presentation rather than take over the presentation.

146
00:12:48,219 --> 00:12:51,020
In other words, if we're presenting the Tenderfoot badge,

147
00:12:51,240 --> 00:12:56,979
we don't want to speak about the history of the Tenderfoot badge from the inception of scouting to the present day.

148
00:12:56,980 --> 00:13:03,200
We want a few brief sentences to explain what it means and then get on with the presentation.

149
00:13:03,860 --> 00:13:11,460
And when I'm talking about uniformity, what I mean is that individual honors are presented in exactly the same way.

150
00:13:11,760 --> 00:13:13,700
Let's stick with the Tenderfoot badge.

151
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:17,020
If we're going to present the Tenderfoot badge to 10 boys,

152
00:13:17,340 --> 00:13:22,780
each one of the boys is going to receive the Tenderfoot badge in the same way as all of his fellow scouts.

153
00:13:23,680 --> 00:13:24,900
I'll give you an example.

154
00:13:24,900 --> 00:13:30,800
Within our Court of Honor script, the senior patrol leader calls each one of them by name,

155
00:13:31,220 --> 00:13:32,300
They come up front,

156
00:13:32,439 --> 00:13:33,199
They form a line,

157
00:13:33,380 --> 00:13:34,620
He presents the badge to them,

158
00:13:34,920 --> 00:13:35,780
He salutes them,

159
00:13:36,280 --> 00:13:39,340
He asks for applause and then dismisses them back to their seats.

160
00:13:39,920 --> 00:13:43,500
It's brief and it's simple and it's meaningful to scouts

161
00:13:44,540 --> 00:13:47,079
And it works extraordinarily well.

162
00:13:48,939 --> 00:13:52,280
Predictability is an important point to consider.

163
00:13:52,280 --> 00:13:58,300
I know sometimes we want to change things up and we want the Court of Honor to be different and we want things to.

164
00:13:58,860 --> 00:14:02,319
you know, we want to spice it up a little bit and have some changes and things.

165
00:14:02,439 --> 00:14:08,640
But I'll tell you I'm a very big proponent of these ceremonies being very predictable

166
00:14:08,839 --> 00:14:10,699
And there's a few different reasons why

167
00:14:11,560 --> 00:14:13,900
Scouts appreciate the predictability of a ceremony.

168
00:14:13,900 --> 00:14:23,180
They don't want to be, you know, all of a sudden called up to do something that they have no idea what it is and they don't really know how to do it

169
00:14:23,800 --> 00:14:26,100
And you know they're going to be embarrassed

170
00:14:26,400 --> 00:14:28,520
And they're very sensitive to that type of thing.

171
00:14:28,959 --> 00:14:34,720
So if they know very predictably well when it's my turn for my badge, I will be called up front and this will happen

172
00:14:34,720 --> 00:14:37,680
And it's going to help them get through the Court of Honor.

173
00:14:38,100 --> 00:14:42,340
And parents are going to be looking for those predictable points in the presentation as well.

174
00:14:42,740 --> 00:14:52,820
So I really want you to think hard about the idea of establishing a Court of Honor, crafting it very well and then using it as a tradition within your troop.

175
00:14:52,820 --> 00:14:58,780
So we've got the same Court of Honor script and we use the same Court of Honor script that we've had for probably a dozen years now,

176
00:14:58,900 --> 00:15:01,400
And each time they see it they know what to expect.

177
00:15:01,800 --> 00:15:07,340
Since a Court of Honor is being presented with honor as the theme, it needs to be fairly solemn.

178
00:15:08,420 --> 00:15:17,500
I'm not saying that it should not have any humor injected into it at all, but the humor should be punctuations in the sentence and not the sentence itself, if you understand what I'm saying.

179
00:15:17,500 --> 00:15:25,000
There's going to be inevitable mistakes and omissions, and these need to be anticipated and handled in kind of an unruffled, professional manner.

180
00:15:25,280 --> 00:15:31,200
What we don't want to do is break the idea of we are honoring Scouts and their achievement.

181
00:15:31,740 --> 00:15:39,540
We don't want to make it into something that's just a big joke or a big thing that the Scouts can, as I said before, just kind of laugh up their sleeves at.

182
00:15:39,540 --> 00:15:48,180
In a troop with a good tradition of youth leadership, the youth leaders are much more responsible for the achievements that are being presented than are adults.

183
00:15:48,579 --> 00:15:57,680
Adults should have very much a background role in making these presentations and it should be clear who runs the Scout troop.

184
00:15:58,599 --> 00:16:04,160
So a brief, uncluttered, professional, predictable and meaningful presentation is going to be appreciated by everybody.

185
00:16:04,160 --> 00:16:15,780
Absolutely, Adding things, adding unit-based awards and things like that, that's great, but if they're so complicated to try and figure out and very complex, they're not going to last very long.

186
00:16:16,280 --> 00:16:25,680
And I'll tell you as soon as you add a unit-based award or some kind of special award that you come up with to a court of honor, people are going to expect to see it there each and every time,

187
00:16:25,800 --> 00:16:27,579
And I tell you that's out of hard experience.

188
00:16:27,580 --> 00:16:35,780
So be cautious of creating something that will ultimately demand so much time and effort that it becomes unsupportable in the future.

189
00:16:35,920 --> 00:16:37,320
That's just my advice.

190
00:16:38,060 --> 00:16:39,440
Courts of honor are a wonderful time.

191
00:16:40,100 --> 00:16:48,500
If you think about these guidelines, I think it will help you shape one that is both meaningful and honors the achievement of Scouting

192
00:16:48,500 --> 00:16:52,960
and also helps to cement the idea of exactly why we are Scouting.

193
00:16:52,960 --> 00:17:22,940
Thank you,

